      "reasons": [
        "The definition’s core characterization (“bringing a mental or emotional condition into a form that can be deliberately produced and studied in an experiment”) is not explicitly stated in the packet; the packet only says emotional conditions are “exceedingly difficult” to bring “under experimental control,” without defining “experimental control” in those terms.",
        "The packet does support that emotional states/conditions are difficult to place under experimental control, but it does not support the added phrasing about “deliberately produced and studied” as the meaning of the term.",
        "The definition treats “experimental control” as a general mechanism, while the packet discusses a specific finding: only one class of emotional conditions was found available, induced by reading/recitation/listening to emotionally charactered prose or poetry. The definition does not reflect that specificity."
